== 4. Of the Contract

Marriage has two distinct parts: the first is its beginning; the second, its accomplishment or consummation.

The beginning is the contract, or betrothal; the end or accomplishment is the solemn declaration of the contract, by what we properly call marriage. Deut 20:7: “Has any man become betrothed to a woman and not married her? Let him return home, or he may die in battle and another man marry her.” Matt 1:18: “His mother Mary was betrothed to Joseph, but before they came together, she was found to be with child through the Holy Spirit.”

Between the contract and the marriage there ought to be some certain space or distance of time. The reasons for this may be these:

First, a business of such great importance as this should not be attempted rashly or unadvisedly, but should rather be done by degrees, in process of time, according to that saying, not so ancient as true:#footnote[Diu deliberandum quod semel statuendum [Long must be the deliberating over what is to be settled once for all].] #emph[Actions of weight require mature deliberation before resolution.] Secondly, that during such a space inquiry may be made whether there is any just cause that may hinder the consummation of the marriage. For before the parties come together, what is amiss may be remedied and amended, which will be too late to do afterward. Thirdly, in these cases persons betrothed must have regard to decency as well as to necessity: not seeking at once, upon the contract, to satisfy their own fleshly desires after the manner of brute beasts, but proceeding in it with mature deliberation.

The contract is a declaration, or mutual promise, of future marriage, made before fit and qualified judges and witnesses.

The best way of giving this promise is to make it#footnote[In verbis de praesenti.] #emph[in words concerning the present time,] and simply, without any exception or condition, expressed or conceived. For by this means the bond is made the surer, and the ground or foundation of the future marriage is better laid. And it is on this ground alone that persons betrothed are called husband and wife in Scripture. Jacob, speaking of Rachel, who was only betrothed to him, says to Laban, “Grant me my wife” (Gen 29:21). Deut 22:23: “If there is a virgin betrothed to a man, and another man encounters her in the city and sleeps with her, you#super[+] must take both of them out to the gate of that city and stone them to death—the young woman because she did not cry out in the city, and the man because he has violated his neighbor’s wife.” Matt 1:20: “Joseph … do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife.”

Now if the promise is uttered in words#footnote[In futurum.] #emph[for time to come,] it does not strictly bind the parties to performance. For example, if one of them says to the other, #emph[N., I will take you,] etc., and not #emph[N., I do take you,] etc., by this form of speech the match is #emph[not made, but only promised] to be made afterward. But if, on the other hand, it is said, #emph[N., I do take you,] and not #emph[I will take you,] by these terms the marriage is begun at that very instant: though#footnote[Non de facto, de iure.] not in regard of fact, yet in regard of the right and claim that the parties have in each other, in deed and in truth. And this is the common opinion of the learned. Yet nevertheless, if the contracting parties say to each other, #emph[I will take you to,] etc., with the intention of binding themselves at the present, the bond is strictly made in conscience before God, and so the contract is indeed made#footnote[De praesenti.] #emph[for the present] time before God. It is true that he who keeps the promise he has made, as far as lies in him, does well. Yet if the promise has or carries with it some just cause why he should afterward change his purpose, the contract expressed in terms for time to come, though it was made earlier and confirmed by oath, must nevertheless give place to the contract made for time present.

Betrothals made on a condition that is honorable, possible, and belonging to marriage cease or remain pending just so far as the condition attached ceases or remains pending. For example, one party may promise to marry the other on condition that his or her kinsman consents to the match, or on condition of a dowry that she is to bring him, suitable to her upbringing and the family she comes from. As these conditions are kept or not kept, the promise likewise stands or does not stand.

But conditions that are necessarily understood, or that can certainly be kept and come to pass, neither hinder nor suspend marriage. Such as this: #emph[I promise you marriage, if I live;] or, #emph[I promise to marry you, if the sun rises tomorrow.]

Again, conditions that are either impossible or dishonorable are not to be counted as promises attached#footnote[In fauorem Matrimonij [in favor of marriage].] in the way of marriage. Of this sort are these: #emph[I will be your wife, if you will take wings and fly;] or, #emph[If you will bring about a miscarriage.]

Furthermore, if the betrothed parties lie together before the condition (though honorable and belonging to marriage) is performed, then the contract for time to come is, without further controversy, sure and certain. For where there has been carnal use of each other’s body, it is always presupposed that mutual consent concerning marriage has gone before.
